WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.710
Good morning, John. Beginnings and endings...

00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:05.380
I like it. I like this frame.

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:08.760
I love beginnings. I've got several beginnings
that I'm interested in.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:13.150
Soda syrups. Sock - anything to do with socks
I'm really into right now.

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:17.080
I've got a couple things I'd like to end on
a sort of a national level.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:21.500
But in terms of me, things that I can definitely
do and control, I don't know what I want to end.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:25.140
We interview people for jobs sometimes, sometimes
for fairly high-level jobs.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.682
I once had somebody come into a job interview
and be like,

00:00:27.682 --> 00:00:31.750
“I could get you to acquisition
in just a couple of years,”

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:33.500
and I was like, “To ac - to what?”

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.620
Like, building Complexly up to sell it, and
I was just like,

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:40.580
“Oh no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no,
no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no,

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:42.579
I wanna be running this company when I die!”

00:00:42.579 --> 00:00:44.440
I was really sounding like a moped there
for a second.

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:48.880
Here's a thing that I would like to end.
I would like to end my line edits for my book.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:55.530
What are line edits, Hank? Okay, here's what book writing
looks like, if you're in the traditional publishing industry.

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:58.870
First, you write a book.
I don't know how this works. I don't know.

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:04.400
So you've got a manuscript, and you've got
an editor. However that happened... is a mystery.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:08.259
But you got it, and you give it to the editor.
And then either they tell you, "This is trash,"

00:01:08.259 --> 00:01:15.000
or they write you a letter. And that's called
the editorial letter, and it says, “This book is not trash,”

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:21.960
followed by a 22 page list of all the ways in which
the book is trash. This is the success condition.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:26.539
So then, you finished your big revision.
You've basically rewritten the book.

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:31.100
There's 50,000 words that aren't in there
anymore and 40,000 new ones.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:39.300
Then you get your line edits. And your line edits are like,
line by line, your editor goes, “Why did you do this?”

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.859
Usually it's fairly simple things like it's,
you need a different word here

00:01:42.859 --> 00:01:45.619
because you used this word earlier in the
sentence beforehand.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:49.960
And then occasionally you get a line edit
that basically says, “Rewrite this chapter,”

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:51.520
and you're like, “This isn't a line edit!”

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:54.849
And during the line edit is for me
when I read the entire book out loud.

00:01:54.849 --> 00:01:58.079
I'm a slow reader regardless, but reading
out loud is particularly slow.

00:01:58.079 --> 00:02:03.849
Now of course, after your line edit, you get your copy
edit, and that's when somebody, in my case last time,

00:02:03.849 --> 00:02:09.030
the valedictorian of my high school graduating
class for some reason, Mary Beth Constant,

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:13.700
just reads your entire manuscript and tells
you all of the little things you got wrong

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:17.760
and that's usually much less troublesome.
You're just being like,

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:22.719
“Yes, I agree that there should be a comma there.
You're right, I did use the wrong word, Mary Beth.

00:02:22.719 --> 00:02:25.610
Yes, you are still smarter than me
all of these years later.”

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:30.240
This is maybe the weirdest coincidence of
my entire life. I hope I get Mary Beth again.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:36.580
There was literally a single typo in the whole
book when it came out, which is amazing.

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:42.700
So at this point, you've been changing this
book like every day for six, eight months?

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:46.140
You've been looking at it and changing things,
and then they say, “Don't change anything else!”

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:52.940
And your entire mind is built to change the
book, and then you have to say, “No!”

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:59.120
This is the final! You decide now that all of
the words that are in here deserve to be in here,

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:02.980
and none of the words that aren't in there
should be, and...

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:04.280
Gaaaaah!

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:08.960
I'm not a high stress person, I'm really not.
I'm laid back, but that is too much.

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:14.360
That is the biggest ending I'm working toward
in 2020, and I'm kind of terrified of it!

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:20.600
I like this book a lot. It's… it's much more ambitious
than my first book, and I think that it says

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:25.040
a lot of interesting and important things
that we need to be saying and thinking about right now.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:29.780
But this is one of the great things about
creative work, that you are forced to an end.

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:33.160
So I guess I'm lucky to have a job where I
get lots of little “I did it” moments,

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:36.800
whether it's finishing a book or
 whether it's finishing this video.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:38.440
John, I'll see you...

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:40.940
right now, because we're on tour together.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:43.730
[JOHN:] Hank, you just made
a whole video about your book

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:48.260
in which you didn't say the name of the book,
which is A Beautifully Foolish Endeavor.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:50.880
Nor did you say when it comes out,
which is July 7th.

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:54.140
And nor did you say that it's available
for pre-order now, which it is.

